 we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡((-x)^{4}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d074f739-fbb3-5d0d-a6b6-adbb55826957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{10}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328416} {:answer/id #uuid "b35badc9-cc23-5c66-9272-b4414dcf6b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{10}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328417} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbc2d0b-2152-5282-966e-613708b259ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328418} {:answer/id #uuid "65df433c-f9c4-5d0d-9dba-a073e32a8e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328419} {:answer/id #uuid "49e824a2-8c83-5e2f-bdbc-b7b2b279f394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65df433c-f9c4-5d0d-9dba-a073e32a8e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328419} :answer-field/id #uuid "69711ccb-86da-5dd0-b54c-611b81d4e05e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d29353e-115f-52b4-80fa-b9c7fdd3563f" :question/math-academy-id "q-88586" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-5}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{4}\\,{e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4341a4f4-22e7-5e46-9103-ba2e52eaa542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328423} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ed1d6e-ff2e-5a98-915b-bb0d19345642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328424} {:answer/id #uuid "429f604a-2d0e-5639-b1e3-05045449a1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328425} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0abe02-949b-563c-ab66-764529873670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328426} {:answer/id #uuid "7d732af2-2633-53a4-ab0d-eb2ed7133d53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4341a4f4-22e7-5e46-9103-ba2e52eaa542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328423} :answer-field/id #uuid "a42e4e26-511f-5e89-99fb-5a2c86787204" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64adcc49-a78d-5de5-9601-bc19735c1f25" :question/math-academy-id "q-88591" :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "972c56a5-0720-5e47-b552-4d54d54b1a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328429} {:answer/id #uuid "b8756552-d2eb-5c1b-a242-ec93a000964c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328430} {:answer/id #uuid "371a0447-4fb9-50a4-b833-58a3086c4600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328431} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5523a0-1d43-5862-b2af-7ef46a3226e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328432} {:answer/id #uuid "95871a77-f1a7-5e2b-b901-0fa07a7f78dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95871a77-f1a7-5e2b-b901-0fa07a7f78dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328433} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a83d34-b0ce-5514-8528-8e23fa285f98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "96edbb01-df4a-527f-b87f-1a45a2a9187b" :question/math-academy-id "q-88686" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={sin}^{2}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
